Q: .NET 10 can&#39;t resolve .MapGroup method in web application A: <p>MapGroup doesnt accept overloading try instead initializing the MapGroup after that adding endpoint such that it is like this</p>
<pre><code>var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

var apiGroup = app.MapGroup(&quot;/api&quot;) 
apiGroup.MapGet(&quot;/&quot;, () =&gt; &quot;Hello World!&quot;)

app.Run();
</code></pre>
<p>hope this help</p>

Q: ViewControllerRegistry register view with model A: <p><code>ViewControllers</code> are only meant for forwarding requests to views directly. If you wish to use a <code>Model</code> and render an actual view with parameters, without using annotated controllers, you can use the functional model in Spring MVC.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc-functional.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc-functional.html</a> Functional endpoints appear to simply register an HTTP interface and cannot be directly associated with the view template.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure where you read that but this isn't what functional endpoints are about. See an example for a handler function that renders a blog post view with a model. You can register many of those into <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc-functional.html#webmvc-fn-router-functions" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RouterFunctions</code></a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>HandlerFunction&lt;ServerResponse&gt; renderBlogPost =
                request -&gt; {
                    Blog blog = findBlog(request.param(&quot;id&quot;));
                    return ServerResponse.ok().render(&quot;showBlog&quot;, Map.of(&quot;blog&quot;,blog));
                };
</code></pre>
